{"thought":"The test passed successfully, indicating that the command can be executed without raising errors, even with the multi-line help text. However, I still need to ensure that the expected completions are generated correctly. I will finalize the test by adding assertions to check for the presence of the '--at' option in the completions, while keeping the error handling for the multi-line help text. This will ensure that the test fails if the bug is still present after the fix.","final_test":"import pytest
import click


def _get_completions(cli, args, incomplete):
    from click.shell_completion import ShellComplete
    comp = ShellComplete(cli, {}, cli.name, "_CLICK_COMPLETE")
    return comp.get_completions(args, incomplete)


def test_multiline_help_option():
    @click.command()
    @click.option(
        "--at",
        type=(str, str),
        multiple=True,
        help="\b\nAttachment with explicit mimetype,\n--at image.jpg image/jpeg"
    )
    def cli(at):
        pass

    # Simulate the command line input for completion
    completions = _get_completions(cli, ["--"], "")
    assert completions is not None
    assert any(c.value.startswith('--at') for c in completions), "Expected '--at' to be in completions"

    # Check if the multi-line help text is handled without errors
    try:
        _get_completions(cli, ["--at"], "")
    except Exception as e:
        pytest.fail(f"Completion raised an exception: {e}")"}
